{"chapter": 2, "sha256": "dc7369d8dbfcbfd86854ae6f38f9e91b96853ecc2138f464c1332b940b13a25a", "voice": {"voice_id": "ppLqTilh7rH7fbUVlXsf", "name": "David - Documentary", "model_id": "eleven_v3", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.5, "similarity_boost": 0.8, "style": 0.0, "use_speaker_boost": true}}, "source": "docs/script.md", "files": {"narration": {"file": "ch2.mp3", "text": "Look at the head. It wears a Fourth Dynasty king's headcloth, it is better kept than the body, and it is too small for it. Nobody disputes that.\n\nHancock, Bauval and Schoch say the figure was a lion first. Rain wore its head away, and a king of the pyramid age had a royal face cut into what was left. You can only carve a smaller form inside a larger one, so the new head came out small. Egyptologists say the head is small because the rock is harder there.\n\nThe equinox sun rises almost due east in every age, so the gaze dates nothing. But the stars behind it drift. About 10,500 BCE, Hancock and Bauval calculate, the constellation Leo rose in the east just before the equinox sun. Egyptologists add that Leo is a Mesopotamian figure, late in Egypt.\n\nIf the lion is that old, they argue, so are its temples.", "seconds": 58.69, "alignment": {"characters": ["L", "o", "o", "k", " ", "a", "t", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "h", "e", "a", "d", ".", " ", "I", "t", " ", "w", "e", "a", "r", "s", " ", "a", " ", "F", "o", "u", "r", "t", "h", " ", "D", "y", "n", "a", "s", "t", "y", " ", "k", "i", "n", "g", "'", "s", " ", "h", "e", "a", "d", "c", "l", "o", "t", "h", ",", " ", "i", "t", " ", "i", "s", " ", "b", "e", "t", "t", "e", "r", " ", "k", "e", "p", "t", " ", "t", "h", "a", "n", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "b", "o", "d", "y", ",", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "i", "t", " ", "i", "s", " ", "t", "o", "o", " ", "s", "m", "a", "l", "l", " ", "f", "o", "r", " ", "i", "t", ".", " ", "N", "o", "b", "o", "d", "y", " ", "d", "i", "s", "p", "u", "t", "e", "s", " ", "t", "h", "a", "t", ".", "\n", "\n", "H", "a", "n", "c", "